WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.940
Thanks for joining me on fascinating frontiers, episode 127 coming to you on July 10, 2026.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.020
Here's what's happening in space and science today.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:16.260
A Chinese rocket just became only the second booster ever recovered after an orbital launch,

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.800
landing on a barge after sending its payload skyward.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:28.180
The maiden flight of China's Long March 10B lifted off at 0415 UTC on July 10 from Hainan.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:33.220
Its Carollox first stage landed successfully on a downrange barge.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:38.100
The Methalox second stage placed an unidentified payload into orbit.

00:00:38.680 --> 00:00:45.580
This achievement places China alongside only one other nation with demonstrated orbital stage reuse capability.

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.320
Additional recovery attempts are planned for upcoming flights.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:56.280
Now, shifting focus from reusable hardware on Earth to the infrared view of a distant galaxy collision.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:02.500
The James Webb Space Telescope will mark four years of science operations on July 12.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:09.160
Its infrared vision pierced thick dust lanes to expose the starry center of galaxy Centaurus A

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.200
in greater detail than previous observations.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:16.560
The image highlights structures shaped by a past cosmic collision.

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:23.560
Scientists will continue using the telescope to map similar dust obscured regions across nearby galaxies.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:31.000
Next data releases are expected to include additional early universe targets observed during the same cycle.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:37.000
From galactic collisions to the faint particles left by stellar deaths across cosmic time.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:44.760
Researchers report the first possible signals of neutrinos produced by supernova explosions over billions of years.

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:52.820
These ghost particles travel vast distances and interact so weekly that only rare underground detectors can register them.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:57.940
The signals appear as a diffuse background rather than individual events.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:04.340
Confirmation would link current observations directly to the cumulative history of stellar deaths.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.820
Follow-up analyses of detector data are already underway.

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:15.960
A single supernova can release roughly 10 to the power of 58 neutrinos in a burst lasting seconds,

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:20.200
yet these particles slip through entire planets without leaving a trace.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:25.100
Imagine riding alongside one of them as it exits the collapsing core.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:33.400
In less than a second it crosses the star's outer layers, then spends thousands of years crossing the galaxy at nearly the speed of light,

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.380
while ordinary light and matter lag behind.

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:43.660
Detectors buried kilometers underground register perhaps one interaction per day from the steady rain of neutrinos

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:47.360
that have been arriving from ancient explosions across cosmic time.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:54.340
That background flux carries information about the total number of core collapse events over billions of years,

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:57.900
a census no telescope using light can match.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.880
The remaining puzzle is whether the energy spectrum of these ghost particles will finally reveal

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:07.720
how often the most massive star is exploded in the early universe,

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:12.880
or whether some other still unseen process is also feeding the signal.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.820
Flight controllers at Johns Hopkins Applied Physics Laboratory confirmed new horizons

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:23.760
resumed operations on June 23, after nearly a year in sleep mode.

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:32.080
The spacecraft sits roughly 5.9 billion miles from Earth, where signals take 8 hours and 52 minutes to travel.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:37.800
Instruments will soon downlink heliosphere plasma data and perform health checks.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:43.840
The Venetia Bernie student dust counter continues collecting kuiper belt particle counts.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:49.500
Software upgrades for distant sun operations are being tested through the end of the year.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:55.380
New horizons has returned to active duty after 321 days of hibernation.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:59.700
Its instruments now streaming fresh measurements from the outer heliosphere.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:06.140
The spacecraft's plasma sensors and dust counter continue recording conditions far beyond the planets,

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:11.420
where solar wind interactions weaken and interstellar material begins to dominate.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:18.460
Mission teams will next downlink ultraviolet spectrograph observations of hydrogen gas distribution.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:24.000
With power levels declining and signal travel times stretching past 9 hours,

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:30.320
every downlink becomes a carefully planned window into a region visited by only two other spacecraft.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:35.560
What patterns will the dust detector reveal once the full data set reaches Earth?

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:40.460
From the edge of the solar system to the next flagship telescope heading outward.

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:47.540
NASA selected SpaceX's Falcon Heavy to carry the Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope to orbit this

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:54.420
August. The telescope will serve a wide areas of the sky for exoplanets and dark energy signatures.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:05:00.340
The mission represents one of NASA's next flagship astrophysics projects after web.

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:05.360
Ground teams are completing final integration and testing at the launch site.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:09.820
Launch vehicle certification reviews remain the next major milestone.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:14.820
From upcoming astrophysics missions to the people who will live aboard the station.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:21.440
NASA astronaut Anil Menon will launch a board Soyuz MS-29 on July 14,

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:25.320
with Cosmonaut's Piotr Dubrov and Anna Kikina.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:31.680
The eight-month mission will join the Expedition 74 crew aboard the International Space Station.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:37.520
Menon's assignment continues NASA's participation in long-duration station research.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:43.040
Training for the crew concluded earlier this month at the Gagarin Cosmonaut Training Center.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:47.080
Life coverage of the launch is scheduled through NASA channels.

00:05:47.820 --> 00:05:51.620
From crewed operations to the quiet processes that build stars.

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:57.460
Radio telescopes captured gas motions inside cold, dense press-deller cores,

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:59.400
where stars like the Sun form.

00:06:00.140 --> 00:06:05.760
Gravity holds the cores together while internal motions reveal how material drifts before final

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:12.220
collapse. The observations help clarify why some cores form single stars and others produce

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:19.080
multiples. Specific velocity patterns measured across several cores now constrain formation models.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:23.880
Additional mapping campaigns with the same instruments are scheduled for later this year.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:27.880
From star formation to a Canadian lake seen from orbit.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:34.100
The Copernicus Sentinel-2 mission returned a striking color image of Canada's Great Bear Lake.

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:39.500
The satellite data highlight watercolor variations and surrounding terrain features.

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:45.760
Such observations support ongoing studies of northern lake systems and seasonal ice cover.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:51.160
The image forms part of ESA's regular Earth from Space series.

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:55.460
Additional passes over the same region are planned throughout the summer.

00:06:56.040 --> 00:07:00.120
From Earth imagery to a pioneer who helped open space to more people.

00:07:00.820 --> 00:07:06.160
Aviation pioneer Wally Funk, who became the oldest woman to fly in space, has passed away.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:11.880
She flew aboard a blue origin suborbital flight in 2021 at age 82.

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:18.400
Funk's career included work as one of the Mercury 13 women tested for astronaut fitness in the

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:24.420
1960s. Her flight demonstrated expanded opportunities for private citizens in space.

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:30.080
Memorial statements from the commercial space flight community are expected in coming days.

00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:36.360
From personal legacies to this week's evening sky. The bright pattern of upper Scorpius

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:39.820
appears prominently in the southern sky just after nightfall.

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:45.440
Telescopic observers can target star clusters and nebulae within the constellation.

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:49.080
In the west, Regulus is departing from Venus.

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:53.680
The Big Dipper remains visible to the lower left of Polaris for northern viewers.

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:57.480
Weekly sky updates will continue through July 19.

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:01.540
From naked eye viewing to radar measurements of ground movement.

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:07.780
Radar measurements from the NISA satellite recorded significant ground shifts in La Guira

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:11.580
and surrounding areas after June 2026 earthquakes.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:16.080
The data quantifies surface movement across multiple fault segments.

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:20.720
Such observations help refine models of regional seismic hazard.

00:08:21.620 --> 00:08:24.620
Processing of additional radar scenes is ongoing.

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:29.860
The mission continues routine monitoring of active tectonic zones worldwide.

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:34.880
From Earth monitoring to sounding rocket studies of the Sun Earth connection.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:40.160
Researchers are launching sounding rockets from Alaska to examine solar flares,

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:42.320
auroras and related phenomena.

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:45.740
The state's position, partly inside the Arctic Circle,

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:49.620
provides unique access to high latitude space weather effects.

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:53.320
Data will feed into models of the Sun Earth system.

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:56.840
Multiple flights are scheduled through the remainder of the season.

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:00.800
Results will complement measurements from orbiting observatories.

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:05.280
From near-Earth space weather to broader launch infrastructure plans.

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:10.460
An office within the U.S. Department of the Interior issued a request for information

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:12.840
on offshore orbital launch options.

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:17.240
The effort aims to reduce pressure on existing coastal space ports.

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:22.920
Concepts under consideration include mobile platforms and fixed marine structures.

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:27.660
Responses will inform future regulatory and infrastructure planning.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:31.300
The comment period remains open for industry input.

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:37.140
From U.S. planning to European Space Port growth, Sweden is coordinating with Italy,

00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:42.920
France and Germany, plus European aerospace firms on upgrades at Space Port East range.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:48.220
The project targets growth in commercial launches and new technology demonstrations.

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:53.120
Expanded facilities will support both suborbital and orbital missions.

00:09:53.920 --> 00:09:58.320
Timeline milestones include infrastructure completion within the next several years.

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:01.520
Additional partner announcements are anticipated.

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:06.140
From launch site development to educational views of eclipses elsewhere.

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:10.900
New NASA animations compare solar eclipses across the solar system,

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:13.440
including events on Jupiter and Saturn.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:20.160
The sequences illustrate how different moon sizes and orbital distances produce varied eclips types.

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:25.540
One video addresses the eventual end of total solar eclipses visible from Earth.

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:29.080
The resources are intended for educational use.

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:33.000
Additional planetary eclips visualizations are in development.

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:39.120
Next time, keep an eye on the final integration steps for the Roman Space Telescope ahead of

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:42.780
its August launch. That's fascinating frontiers for today.

